{"builder":"Goal: when a config file holds malformed JSON, the error must name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. The app must still exit 1 with a `bad config:` line on stderr. Do not adopt the suggested fix of catching the error and returning `{}`: `loadConfig` must keep throwing on malformed JSON, and valid configs must load exactly as before.\n\nCurrent code: `src/config.js` `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. `src/app.js` catches any error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1; on success it writes `listening on <port>` to stdout. `test/config.test.js` has two tests that use `node:test`. One of them asserts that bad JSON throws a `SyntaxError`. `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, wrap the `JSON.parse` call only (not `readFileSync`, so missing-file ENOENT errors still go through unchanged) in try/catch. On a parse error, throw a new error whose `message` contains the path exactly as it was passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path) and the line and column of the mistake, for example `<path>:3:3: <original message>` or `<path> (line 3 column 3): ...`. Keep the thrown error a `SyntaxError`, or else update the existing test in step 3. Setting `{ cause: err }` is fine.\n2. Work out the line and column yourself; do not rely only on Node's own message, because some Node versions give only `position N` or nothing at all. If the message gives `line L column C`, use those. Otherwise, if it gives `position N`, count newlines in the text before offset N to get the 1-based line, and take column = N minus the offset just after the last newline, plus 1. Check your result against the target case: the four-line file `{` / `  \"port\": 9000,` / `  greeting: \"x\"` / `}` must report line 3, column 3 (Node v22 gives position 20 there). If neither a position nor a line is available, still include the path.\n3. Leave `src/app.js` printing `bad config: ${error.message}` and exiting 1, so stderr shows the path and location. Change nothing on the success path.\n4. Add tests to `test/config.test.js` that would fail on the current code:\n   - `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws, and the error's `message` includes that file's path.\n   - `loadConfig` on the four-line file above throws an error whose message includes the path and gives line 3, column 3.\n   - Optionally, spawn `node src/app.js <file>` with `child_process.spawnSync` and check: exit status 1, empty stdout, and stderr containing `bad config:`, the path, and the line and column.\n   Keep the existing tests passing: `{\"port\": 9000}` must still load as `{ port: 9000 }`.\n5. Optionally, update the README sentence to say the message names the file and location, keeping `bad config: <message>` and exit 1.\n6. Run `npm test`. Then check by hand: write c.json (the four lines above) to a temp dir and run `node src/app.js $d/c.json`. Expect exit 1, empty stdout, and stderr with `bad config:`, `$d/c.json`, line 3 and column 3. Write ok.json with `{\"port\": 9000}` and run the app on it: expect `listening on 9000` and exit 0.\n\nOut of scope: returning `{}` or the defaults on bad JSON, changing ENOENT reporting (beyond its staying an exit-1 `bad config:` failure), schema or value validation, and JSON5 or comment support.\n\nOpen one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head from the repository root, using the repo's Node (v22). Use a fresh `d=$(mktemp -d)` for each check unless the check says otherwise.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; wc -c < $d/out; cat $d/out`\nPASS: the exit code is 1 and `$d/out` is empty (0 bytes).\nFAIL: any other exit code, or anything on stdout, including a `listening on` line.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: `cat $d/err`, using the same `$d` as Check 1.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the exact string `$d/c.json` as passed on the command line, and gives the mistake's location as line 3 and column 3 (for example `line 3 column 3` or `c.json:3:3`).\nFAIL: any of these is missing; the path is missing or altered; or the location is missing or not line 3, column 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: it prints `OK`.\nFAIL: it prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`, or it crashes.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, {port: 9000}); console.log('OK', JSON.stringify(v))\"`\nPASS: the app prints `listening on 9000` on stdout and exits 0, and the second command prints `OK {\"port\":9000}`.\nFAIL: a different output, a non-zero exit, or an assertion error.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun: `npm test` at the PR head. Then, in a scratch copy or a temporary worktree, take the PR's base commit (for example `git merge-base HEAD <PR base ref>`), copy the PR head's `test/` directory over it (keeping the base's `src/`), and run `npm test` there.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the PR head, and in the base-src run at least one test fails whose assertion checks that the malformed-JSON error message includes the file path. Confirm this by reading the failing test.\nFAIL: `npm test` fails at the head, or no test fails against the base's `src/`, or none of the failing tests asserts that the path is in the error.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- Read `src/config.js` and `src/app.js` to see whether line and column are computed from the parse position or taken only from Node's message text. Relying only on Node's text would break on Node versions that report only a position; note it if so.\n- Note it if `loadConfig` now catches `readFileSync` errors or changes how a missing file (ENOENT) is reported. It should still be an exit-1 `bad config:` failure.\n- Note any change that returns `{}` or the defaults on bad JSON, or that adds JSON5 or comment support or schema validation. These are non-goals."}